#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125282} :activity/id #uuid "4335d62f-6a14-5864-be7f-c04874251357" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125729 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x,y) = x y^2 - x^3 y.$ Then the first-order partial derivatives are $$\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} (x,y) = y^2 -3x^2y \\quad \\mathrm{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x,y) = 2xy -x^3,$$\n\nand the second-order **mixed partial derivatives** are $$\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y\\, \\partial x}(x,y) = 2y -3x^2 \\quad \\mathrm{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y}(x,y) = 2y-3x^2.$$\n\nNotice that these two mixed partial derivatives are equal, i.e., $$\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y\\, \\partial x}=\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y}.$$\n\nIn general, if a function $f(x,y)$ has continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U,$ and the second-order mixed partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $U,$ then the mixed partial derivatives are equal everywhere on $U.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "96f3873b-8f1b-5481-827e-503e557bbe9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2408 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "81952bcf-8d7d-50aa-9c3d-0f75127d5ba3" :step/math-academy-id 10705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125283}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124252 :question/id #uuid "2247dc40-0c71-596c-9da4-a3bffd85e106" :question/math-academy-id "e-3152"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048781}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404121}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "3cac5a52-91a4-530b-93a7-3b880c3d7869" :step/math-academy-id 10709 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125285}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125284 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124253 :question/id #uuid "d45926a5-0126-50b4-bdc7-a9f686408986" :question/math-academy-id "e-3153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403179}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions of Partial Derivatives at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "03eb0268-bd36-5a13-83fe-9c238bbe818f" :step/math-academy-id 10710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125285 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124254 :question/id #uuid "d62e8502-b3c5-5923-8ab3-ef988507cd2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3154"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394064}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "e1813dc3-f991-54bc-bf74-b3e674f858b0" :step/math-academy-id 10713 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125755 :tutorial/content "If $f(x,y)$ is a function whose first-order and second-order mixed partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ then the second-order mixed partial derivatives are equal everywhere on $U.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y\\, \\partial x}\n$$\n\nThis result also extends to higher-order partial derivatives. For example, if the third-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ are also continuous everywhere on $U,$ then the third-order mixed partial derivatives are independent of the order in which the differentiation is performed. \n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{red}\\partial y\\, \\partial x \\, \\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can think of $\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y$ and $\\color{red}\\partial y\\, \\partial x \\, \\partial x$ as distinct permutations of the symbols $\\partial x,\\partial x,$ and $\\partial y.$ Any permutation of these symbols gives the same result. For example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{purple}\\partial x\\, \\partial y\\, \\partial x}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This does **not** mean that *all* third partial derivatives are equal! For example, it is generally true that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y\\, \\partial y}.\n$$\n\n \nThese are not equal because $\\partial x\\, \\partial y\\, \\partial y$ (in the right-hand side denominator) is a permutation of the symbols $\\partial x, \\partial y, \\partial y,$ not $\\partial x, \\partial x, \\partial y.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2267ab49-4a4d-553c-ba46-127edac4a2e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2884 :tutorial/title "Equality of Higher-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "fec110b5-a92e-591e-b9da-4dceefec8548" :step/math-academy-id 15480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125284}}] :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124742}]}
